## ⚠️ Issues & Areas to Improve

### 🔴 Critical Issues

#### 1. Hardcoded Connection String
```csharp
// DIContainer.cs line 47
options.UseSqlServer("Server=BELUGA;Database=ComputerRepairDB;Trusted_Connection=True;...")
```
**Problem:** The connection string is hardcoded with a specific machine name (`BELUGA`). This will fail on any other machine and is a deployment problem. It also becomes a security risk if the project is ever shared publicly.

**Fix:** Load it from [App.config](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/EdisonComputers/App.config) / `appsettings.json`:
```csharp
var connectionString = ConfigurationManager.ConnectionStrings["DefaultConnection"].ConnectionString;
options.UseSqlServer(connectionString);
```

#### 2. Default Admin Password Shown in Plain Text
```csharp
// Program.cs line 80-81
"Username: admin\n" +
"Password: admin123\n\n" +
```
**Problem:** The default password `admin123` is shown in a `MessageBox` on first run. For a real client deployment, this is an acceptable one-time setup flow, but `admin123` is a dangerously weak default. Add a password complexity requirement on the first-login change.

#### 3. [AddUserAsync](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/BLL/Services/UserService/UserService.cs#36-62) Bug
```csharp
// UserService.cs lines 52-60
var newuser = new User { ..., PasswordHash = BCrypt.Net.BCrypt.HashPassword(user.PasswordHash) };
await _userRepository.Add(user); // ← BUG: adds 'user' not 'newuser'
```
**Problem:** You correctly create `newuser` with the hashed password, but then save the original unhashed [user](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/EdisonComputers/Presentation.csproj.user) object. **This would store passwords in plain text.** Should be `_userRepository.Add(newuser)`.

#### 4. Operator Precedence Bug in LINQ ([JobRepository.cs](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/DAL/Repositories/JobRepository/JobRepository.cs) line 175)
```csharp
.Where(j => j.JobStatus == JobStatus.TasksCompleted || 
            j.JobStatus == JobStatus.ReadyForBilling && 
            (j.JobStatus != JobStatus.Cancelled || j.Billing != null || !j.Billing.IsPaid == false))
```
**Problem:** `&&` binds tighter than `||` — the logic is almost certainly wrong because `ReadyForBilling && ...` is evaluated first, then OR'd with `TasksCompleted`. The redundant `j.JobStatus != JobStatus.Cancelled` on a record already matched by the other conditions adds confusion. Parentheses need to be added and the logic simplified.

---

### 🟡 Medium Issues

#### 5. Debug `Console.WriteLine` in Production Code
```csharp
// Repository.cs line 27
Console.WriteLine($"Rows affected: {result}");
```
This should use `LoggingService.LogInformation()` or be removed entirely for production.

#### 6. [InvoiceService.cs](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/BLL/Services/InvoiceService/InvoiceService.cs) Is Empty / Incomplete
```csharp
public class InvoiceService
{
    // method is commented out
}
```
The invoice generation logic is entirely commented out in both [InvoiceService.cs](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/BLL/Services/InvoiceService/InvoiceService.cs) and [JobRepository.cs](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/DAL/Repositories/JobRepository/JobRepository.cs). If the client needs to generate invoices (likely, given the scope), this is a missing feature. The commented-out code is well designed — it just needs to be completed.

#### 7. `IjobRepository` — Naming Convention
The interface is named `IjobRepository` (lowercase 'j') but should be `IJobRepository` (uppercase). Inconsistency with all other interface names (`ICustomerRepository`, `IDeviceRepository`, etc.). This is minor but looks unprofessional.

#### 8. [ValidateCustomer](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/BLL/Services/CustomerService/CustomerService.cs#185-218) is `async` But Has No `await`
```csharp
private async Task ValidateCustomer(Customer customer)
```
This method has no `await` expressions inside it. It should be `private void ValidateCustomer(...)` or return `Task` synchronously via `return Task.CompletedTask`. Causes a compiler warning.

#### 9. `IsPaid` Is `bool?` (Nullable) When It Should Be `bool`
```csharp
public bool? IsPaid { get; set; } = false;
```
A payment is either paid or not. There is no meaningful "unknown" state. This nullable creates null-check noise throughout the codebase (e.g. `j.Billing.IsPaid == true`, `!j.Billing.IsPaid == false`).

#### 10. `WarrantyPeriodinMonths` Name Is Misleading
The field comment says "even tho this says months, it can be days or months based on the WarrantyUnit". The property name should be renamed to `WarrantyPeriod` or `WarrantyDuration` since its unit is variable.

---

### 🟢 Minor/Low Priority

- **[README.md](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/README.md) is empty** (only 17 bytes) — for a client or team project, even a one-paragraph README is valuable.
- **[Class1.cs](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/BLL/Class1.cs) files** exist in both `BLL` and `Core` projects — these are unused scaffold files and should be deleted.
- **Commented-out code** throughout (e.g. global query filters, old property names, old methods) — should be cleaned up or moved to Git history.
- **`recentActovities`** typo in [DashboardService.cs](file:///d:/programming/EdisonComputersProject/EdisonComputers/BLL/Services/DashboardService/DashboardService.cs) line 78 — minor.
- **No unit tests** — `TestProject1` and `EdisonProjectTest01` folders exist but appear empty. Tests for the warranty logic and billing calculations would be valuable.

